(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["cam-nutrition-exam-2-animal-assisted-therapy-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Animal-Assisted Therapy — Set 2 — PA Quizzes","category":"CAM / Nutrition","sem":"summer-1-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which examination format does hippotherapy certification use?","choices":["A practical riding assessment","A multiple-choice examination","An oral board examination","A portfolio of documented cases"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lecture states applicants must have passed a multiple-choice examination, in addition to the practice-hour requirements. — STUDENT Animal-Assisted Therapy Dr. McInnis 2026.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"What training does the law require of an emotional support animal?","choices":["Certification by the Professional Association of Therapeutic Horsemanship International","A minimum of 100 hours of supervised task training","Completion of a professional service animal training program","Training is not required by law"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The lecture states training is not required by law for an emotional support animal, though a licensed healthcare or mental health professional may recommend one and provide supporting documentation. — STUDENT Animal-Assisted Therapy Dr. McInnis 2026.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"How does the lecture define animal-assisted therapy?","choices":["A goal-directed intervention in which the animal is used during treatment to aid achievement of therapeutic goals","Any interaction between a patient and an animal that improves mood","The keeping of a pet for company, amusement, and psychological support","An educational approach that develops leadership and life skills"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. This is the lecture's definition of animal-assisted therapy. — STUDENT Animal-Assisted Therapy Dr. McInnis 2026.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"How does the lecture define equine-facilitated learning?","choices":["A treatment strategy that uses equine movement to reach functional goals","A certification pathway for therapy animal handlers","A mental health treatment addressing psychological disorders","An experiential learning approach that promotes development of life skills for educational, professional and personal goals through equine-assisted activities"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. This is the lecture's definition of equine-facilitated learning. — STUDENT Animal-Assisted Therapy Dr. McInnis 2026.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"How do horses contribute to self-awareness in equine-assisted psychotherapy?","choices":["By responding only to verbal commands, which teaches clear speech","By providing feedback and mirroring, as keen observers of human behaviour sensitive to movement and nonverbal cues","By being unresponsive, which forces the client to self-reflect","By competing with the client, which builds resilience"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lecture describes horses offering feedback and mirroring, being keen observers, vigilant and environmentally aware, and sensitive to movement and nonverbal cues, which promotes self-awareness. — STUDENT Animal-Assisted Therapy Dr. McInnis 2026.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"Who typically delivers an equine-assisted psychotherapy session?","choices":["A riding instructor working alone","A physical therapist using equine movement","A veterinarian supervising the horse's welfare","A mental-health therapist working with an equine specialist"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The lecture states sessions usually include a mental-health therapist working with an equine specialist. — STUDENT Animal-Assisted Therapy Dr. McInnis 2026.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"What does the movement of the horse provide during hippotherapy?","choices":["Bilateral rhythmic stimulation that mimics the human gait when walking","Passive stretching of the lower limbs only","Isometric strengthening of the upper body","Vestibular stimulation without any motor component"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The lecture states hippotherapy provides bilateral rhythmic stimulation to the rider and mimics the gait of a human when walking, with neural and motor pathways benefiting from motor exercises and cyclic motions. — STUDENT Animal-Assisted Therapy Dr. McInnis 2026.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"Are emotional support and comfort animals service animals under Titles II and III of the Americans with Disabilities Act?","choices":["Yes, if the animal is a dog","Yes, provided a licensed professional has written a letter","No, they are expressly not service animals under those titles","Only in healthcare settings"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The lecture states emotional support animals and comfort animals are not service animals under Title II and Title III of the Americans with Disabilities Act, and do not have the same protection. — STUDENT Animal-Assisted Therapy Dr. McInnis 2026.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"Which animals were integrated into the 1867 German community for people with disabilities?","choices":["Only birds, following Nightingale's recommendation","Only horses, for physical rehabilitation","Birds, cats, dogs, and horses","Farm animals and dogs, in a veterans' programme"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The lecture states that in 1867 in Germany, animals including birds, cats, dogs and horses were integrated into a community for those with disabilities. — STUDENT Animal-Assisted Therapy Dr. McInnis 2026.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"What does the lecture say about breed restrictions for service dogs?","choices":["Only retrievers and shepherds qualify as service dogs","A service dog can be any breed, and entry cannot be refused solely because of breed","Breed restrictions apply in housing but not in public accommodations","Breeds over 50 pounds require additional certification"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lecture states a service dog can be any type of dog breed and that a public establishment or landlord may not prohibit entry solely because it is a certain breed. — STUDENT Animal-Assisted Therapy Dr. McInnis 2026.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"What does a hearing or signal dog do?","choices":["Assists with navigation and environmental awareness for people who are blind","Alerts a person with significant hearing loss or deafness when a sound occurs, such as a knock at the door","Detects the onset of psychiatric episodes and lessens their effects","Interrupts repetitive movements with an unobtrusive signal"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lecture defines a hearing or signal dog as trained to alert a person with significant hearing loss or deafness when a sound occurs. — STUDENT Animal-Assisted Therapy Dr. McInnis 2026.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"Which organisations does the lecture name as equine-assisted therapy certifying bodies?","choices":["The American Hippotherapy Association and the Americans with Disabilities Act","The National Center for Complementary and Integrative Health","The Fair Housing Authority and the Department of Transportation","The Professional Association of Therapeutic Horsemanship International and Eagala"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The lecture names PATH International and Eagala as equine-assisted therapy certifying organisations. — STUDENT Animal-Assisted Therapy Dr. McInnis 2026.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"What skill did the medical-education equine programmes described in the lecture aim to improve?","choices":["Surgical dexterity and fine motor control","Diagnostic reasoning under time pressure","Nonverbal communication skills and bedside manner","Pharmacology knowledge retention"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The lecture frames these programmes around improving nonverbal communication skills and bedside manner among healthcare professionals. — STUDENT Animal-Assisted Therapy Dr. McInnis 2026.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"Which three equine-assisted modalities does the lecture name?","choices":["Hippotherapy, equine-assisted psychotherapy, and equine-facilitated learning","Hippotherapy, therapeutic riding, and vaulting","Equine-assisted psychotherapy, goat yoga, and snake massage","Hippotherapy, service animal training, and emotional support animal placement"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The lecture lists these three equine-assisted therapies. — STUDENT Animal-Assisted Therapy Dr. McInnis 2026.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"From which Greek word does hippotherapy take its name?","choices":["Hippo, meaning river animal","Hippos, meaning movement","Hypo, meaning beneath","Hippos, meaning horse"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The lecture notes the Greek word hippos means horse. — STUDENT Animal-Assisted Therapy Dr. McInnis 2026.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"Which patient groups does the lecture give as hippotherapy indications?","choices":["Patients with anxiety, post-traumatic stress disorder, and grief","Patients with functional limitations and disabilities from neuromotor and sensory dysfunction, such as autism, neuromuscular disorders, and cerebral palsy","Corporate teams seeking leadership development","Patients recovering from elective cosmetic surgery"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lecture lists functional limitations and disabilities in patients with neuromotor and sensory dysfunction, giving autism, neuromuscular disorders and cerebral palsy as examples. — STUDENT Animal-Assisted Therapy Dr. McInnis 2026.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"For what purposes does the lecture say a companion animal is kept?","choices":["Delivering therapy under a clinician's treatment plan","Detecting medical events and summoning help","Company, amusement, psychological support, and extrovert display","Qualifying the owner for housing protections"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The lecture lists company, amusement, psychological support and extrovert display as the purposes of a companion animal. — STUDENT Animal-Assisted Therapy Dr. McInnis 2026.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"In the lecture's comparison table, which pairing correctly separates equine-assisted psychotherapy from equine-facilitated learning?","choices":["Psychotherapy has learning goals; facilitated learning has clinical goals","Psychotherapy involves an instructor; facilitated learning involves a therapist","Psychotherapy is mental health treatment addressing psychological disorders with clinical goals; facilitated learning is educational skill development with learning goals","Psychotherapy serves a participant or learner; facilitated learning serves a patient or client"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The lecture's table contrasts mental health treatment versus educational and skill development, therapist versus instructor or facilitator, psychological disorders versus leadership and life skills, clinical versus learning goals, and patient or client versus participant or learner. — STUDENT Animal-Assisted Therapy Dr. McInnis 2026.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"What did the December 2020 Department of Transportation rule change about air travel?","choices":["It required all airlines to accommodate emotional support animals free of charge","It defined a service animal as a dog individually trained to work for a person with a disability, and stopped treating emotional support animals as service animals","It extended service animal status to miniature horses on aircraft","It removed all training requirements for service animals in flight"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lecture summarises the final rule revising the Air Carrier Access Act regulation in exactly these terms. — STUDENT Animal-Assisted Therapy Dr. McInnis 2026.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Which cues might a psychiatric service dog respond to, per the lecture?","choices":["Pacing, tremors, changes in breathing patterns, or repetitive behaviours","Blood glucose changes detected by scent","Rising ambient noise levels in public spaces","Loss of balance while walking on uneven ground"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The lecture lists these as cues, with responses including nudging the handler, retrieving medication, or guiding the handler to a safe location. — STUDENT Animal-Assisted Therapy Dr. McInnis 2026.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"Under the Fair Housing Act, what may a landlord NOT do when a tenant has a qualifying emotional support animal with a letter?","choices":["Charge a pet fee, enforce breed restrictions, deny housing, or evict the renter because of the animal","Ask whether the animal is required because of a disability","Require the animal to be leashed in common areas","Request a copy of the emotional support animal letter"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The lecture lists exactly these four things a landlord is not allowed to do. — STUDENT Animal-Assisted Therapy Dr. McInnis 2026.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"What does a guide dog or Seeing Eye dog do?","choices":["Alerts to sounds a deaf handler cannot hear","Detects the onset of panic attacks and anxiety episodes","Assists individuals who are blind or have visual impairments with navigation and environmental awareness","Provides emotional comfort and companionship without task training"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The lecture defines the guide dog or Seeing Eye dog in exactly these terms. — STUDENT Animal-Assisted Therapy Dr. McInnis 2026.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"Which species does the lecture say may serve as an emotional support animal?","choices":["Dogs only, under federal rules","Dogs and miniature horses only","Usually a cat or dog, but possibly a turtle, horse, or rabbit","Any animal except reptiles"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The lecture states an emotional support animal is usually a cat or dog but may be a turtle, horse or rabbit. — STUDENT Animal-Assisted Therapy Dr. McInnis 2026.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"What did Florence Nightingale recommend in 1860, according to the lecture?","choices":["Structured horseback riding for physical rehabilitation","Farm animal work for wounded and disabled veterans","Certification requirements for therapy animal handlers","Small companion animals such as birds as an adjunct to healing, especially for the chronically ill"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The lecture credits Nightingale in 1860 with recommending small companion animals, birds specifically, as an adjunct to healing, especially for the chronically ill. — STUDENT Animal-Assisted Therapy Dr. McInnis 2026.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"What do studies suggest equine-assisted psychotherapy may improve in traumatised youth?","choices":["Gross motor coordination and balance","Trust and secure attachment","Academic test performance","Medication adherence"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lecture notes studies suggest equine-assisted psychotherapy may improve trust and secure attachment in traumatised youth. — STUDENT Animal-Assisted Therapy Dr. McInnis 2026.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"Which use of animals in therapy does the lecture date to 600 BC?","choices":["Companionship with dogs for soldiers with post-traumatic stress disorder","Florence Nightingale recommending small companion animals for the chronically ill","Integration of animals into a German community for people with disabilities","Ancient Greeks documenting horse-assisted therapy to raise the spirits of the incurably ill"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The lecture dates horse-assisted therapy to raise the spirits of the incurably ill to the ancient Greeks at 600 BC. — STUDENT Animal-Assisted Therapy Dr. McInnis 2026.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"How does the Americans with Disabilities Act define a service animal?","choices":["A dog individually trained to do work or perform tasks for the benefit of an individual with a disability","Any animal that provides comfort to a person with a disability","A dog or cat certified by a licensed mental health professional","Any trained animal accepted by the establishment being entered"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The lecture gives this definition and notes the disability may be physical, sensory, psychiatric, intellectual or other mental disability. — STUDENT Animal-Assisted Therapy Dr. McInnis 2026.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"Who may write an emotional support animal letter, per the lecture?","choices":["A licensed professional, either a physician or a licensed mental health professional","Any landlord or property manager","A certified animal trainer","The Florida Department of Health"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The lecture states the letter is written by a licensed professional, a physician or licensed mental health professional, documenting the need for the animal as part of mental health treatment. — STUDENT Animal-Assisted Therapy Dr. McInnis 2026.pptx, Slide 10"}]};
